  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 157 PM CDT Sun Aug 2 2026\n\nToday, a surface ridge of high pressure is building into the CWA. \nIn addition, a upper level ridge is extending from the southwestern \nCONUS northeastward into the northern Plains. This leaves the local \narea on the eastern periphery of this feature but is providing \nmodest height rises. This coupled with the relaxation of yesterday's \nCAA will allow highs to rise into the mid 80s. Tomorrow, the surface \nridge will slide east of the area with weak WAA getting underway. As \nit does the upper ridge residing west of the area today will slide \neastward and extend from the southwestern CONUS into the central \nPlains and into the Upper Midwest. This will provide further height \nrises across the area, and this, coupled with the weak WAA will \ndrive highs into the mid to upper 80s. Tomorrow night into Tuesday a \nupper level trough will dig into the northern Plains. This will act \nto flatten the upper level ridge and will force an attendant weak \ncold front into the Upper Midwest and central Plains. Continued Weak \nWAA ahead of this front on Tuesday will allow highs to warm into the \nupper 80s to lower 90s. \n\nTuesday night into Wednesday the aforementioned cold front will sag \ninto the forecast area bringing shower and thunderstorm chances to \nthe northern CWA (40-75%). Showers are expected to diminish late \nWednesday morning into Wednesday afternoon but redevelopment is \nforecast in the evening and is expected to continue thru the \novernight with most areas likely (50-80%) to receive precipitation. \nA few strong storms may be possible with MUCAPE values between 1500-\n2500J/Kg and effective sheer between 45-50kts. Highs Wednesday will \nrange from the upper 70 to lower 80s north where the cold front will \npass earlier in the day to the lower 90s south which will remain \nahead of the front. The latter portion of the forecast period \nThursday through Saturday will feature quasi-zonal flow aloft with \nweak shortwaves moving through the region making occasional \nscattered convection possible through that stretch. A gradual \nwarming trend is expected through that period as well with highs \nThursday in the 80s before rising to the upper 80s to lower 90s by \nSaturday.",
